<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Picquigny</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Picquigny is a small town on the Somme in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picardy">Picardy</a>, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. In 942 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-longsword">William Longsword</a>, ruler of Normandy, was murdered there by men of Arnulf I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> during a meeting on an island in the river. In 1475 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-iv-of-england">Edward IV of England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xi">Louis XI of France</a> signed the Treaty of Picquigny in the town, ending Edward's invasion of France.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/picquigny.jpg" alt="Picquigny" width="320" height="431" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Markus3 (Marc ROUSSEL) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Picquigny_%C3%A9glise_et_rempart_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Picquigny is a commune in the Somme department in Hauts-de-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, on the banks of the river Somme about 13 kilometres north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amiens">Amiens</a>, downstream. It lies at the junction of the N235, D141 and D3 roads, and its station has rail connections to Amiens and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbeville">Abbeville</a>. It was already a Gallic settlement before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> arrived, and a Gallo-Roman cemetery was found in 1895 in the area called Les Vignes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>On 17 December 942 Arnulf I, Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>, and William I, Duke of Normandy, came to Picquigny to sign a peace treaty. Having both left their armies behind, they met on a small island in the river Somme. William left after the meeting and was then assassinated by Arnulf's supporters.</p><p>By the 14th century the town had commune status and a county judge. In 1307 several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-templar">Templars</a> were arrested and imprisoned in the dungeons of the château by order of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-iv-of-france">Philip IV of France</a>. In 1475 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-iv-of-england">Edward IV of England</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> to claim the French throne, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xi">Louis XI</a> bought him off at the Treaty of Picquigny with an annual payment of fifty thousand crowns and a promise not to support his domestic enemies. Edward then withdrew his army. In 1547 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-france">Henry II of France</a> established a market there, to be held on every second Monday of the month.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Château de Picquigny was built around the beginning of the 11th century and rebuilt over the following centuries. It was severely damaged in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a>, but its remains are still imposing. Outside the ramparts stand other medieval buildings, among them a prison, a kitchen, two cellars and the Pavillon Sévigné. The church, originally the château's chapel, stands inside the ramparts behind the ruins, and its roof was destroyed in a fire early in the 20th century. Warmund of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader</a> who became Latin Patriarch of Jerusalem in the early 12th century, is the town's notable native.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Picquigny” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>William Longsword, son of Rollo and ruler of Normandy since about 927, was ambushed and killed by men of Arnulf I, Count of Flanders, at a peace conference at <b>Picquigny</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-17">17 December in history</a></li></ul></section>
